00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.550 align:start position:0%
this muddy wasteland of broken homes and  ash covered crops lies in the shadow of  indonesia's mount samiro volcano it  thundered to life on saturday shooting  clouds of ash into the sky  swallowing homes and blanketing entire  villages in grey  survivors say there was no warning and  that many people were killed  after the eruption my house was damaged  the ceiling destroyed i opened the door  it was so hot so i closed the door went  into the house but then i decided to  flee  many are now sheltering in evacuation  centers where they're given hot meals  and a temporary roof over their head  but indonesia's president says a more  long-term plan is necessary joko widodo  visited the disaster zone on tuesday his  government will now look into moving  homes away from the volcano because of  the threat of more eruptions  we hope that once everything has  subsided that everything can start  fixing infrastructure or even relocating  those from places we predict are too  dangerous to return to just now i  received a report saying that around  2000 families need to be relocated  immediately  mount samaro is the tallest mountain on  the island of java and one of nearly 130  active volcanoes in indonesia  people are being told not to travel  within five kilometres of the crater  because the air nearby is highly  polluted  evacuees complain about coughs brought  on by the dust and smoke while many in  hospital are suffering from burns from  the searing hot lava  when i came down from my truck i stepped  in the lava i ran and climbed onto the  roof of someone's house the volcanic ash  and smoke was choking i felt like i  couldn't breathe for 10 or 15 minutes i  don't know but thank god i was given a  chance to live  others weren't so lucky the body of a 13  year old boy was retrieved on monday the  rescuers are battling dangerous  conditions rain has turned debris into  thick mud and the lava is hardening  for now once bustling villages remain  empty and silent  people say they're too scared to go home  in case the volcano roars to life once  more  alex o'brien al jazeera
 

